-- title:
--   Common chart, stabiliser and valuative cover at the ends
-- statement:
--   The data are as follows. A prime $q$ and a positive integer $M'$ with $q \nmid M'$; a prime $\ell$ with $\ell \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ (hypothesis `hℓ12`) and $\ell \mid M'$ (hypothesis `hℓM'`); a field $L$ of characteristic $0$ and an element $\zeta \in L$ which is a primitive $q$-th root of unity (`hζ`), together with the hypothesis `hι` that there is a ring homomorphism $\iota : L \to \mathbb{C}$ with $\iota(\zeta) = \exp(2\pi i/q)$. A subgroup $H_1 \le (\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times$ is fixed by `hH₁` to be the intersection of [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH q M'`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelJacobian.html#L22), the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/q)^\times$, with the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/\ell)^\times$; thus $H_1$ consists of the units congruent to $1$ modulo $q$ and modulo $\ell$. An intermediate field $K$ of $L \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}(L)$ is fixed by `hK` to be [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.xHFunctionField (q ^ 2 * M') H₁)`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), i.e. the subfield generated over $L$ by the image, under the coefficientwise embedding [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81), of the rational $q$-expansion function field of $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$.
--
--   Next, $A$ is a discrete valuation domain which is henselian, has algebraically closed residue field, is an $L$-algebra with $L$ as fraction field, and for which $q$ lies in the maximal ideal (`hAq`) and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A$ in $L$ (`hζA`); $K$ is an $A$-algebra compatibly with $L$. An element $j \in K$, nonzero, has Laurent expansion [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81) (hypothesis `hj`), the $q$-expansion of the modular $j$-invariant. An element $\varpi \in A$ generates the maximal ideal (`hϖ`), and $\varpi_t \in A$ satisfies $\varpi_t^{\,q^2-1} = q \cdot u$ for some unit $u$ (`hϖt`).
--
--   Write $C :=$ `chartAlgFin A ↥K j` for the $A$-subalgebra of $K$ of elements integral over $A[j]$, and `jChartFin A ↥K j` for $j$ viewed in $C$. An ideal $y \subseteq C$ is assumed maximal (`hy`) and to contain $\varpi$ (`hϖy`), and `hss` asserts that $y$ is supersingular: for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi : C \to \Omega$ with kernel $y$, the value $\varphi(j)$ lies in [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7), the set of elements $j_0$ such that every elliptic Weierstrass curve over $\Omega$ with $j$-invariant $j_0$ has no nonzero point killed by $q$.
--
--   Let $X :=$ [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel A ↥K j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L236), the pushout of $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the two chart algebras (the $j$-finite chart $C$ and the $j^{-1}$-chart) along the middle scheme, with its structure morphism `toBase` to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and the morphism `ιFin` from `XFin` $= \operatorname{Spec} C$.
--
--   The hypothesis `hArig` is the Drinfeld-chart input at every supersingular point. It requires: for every point $z$ of $X$, every germ $\varpi_z$ at $z$ of the image of $\varpi$ under `toBase` (condition `hϖz`) lying in the maximal ideal of the stalk (`hz`), and every point $y'$ of `XFin` with `ιFin` mapping $y'$ to $z$ (`hy'`) and with $y'$ supersingular in the sense above (`hss'`), there exist a complete discrete valuation domain $W$, a ring homomorphism $\sigma : A \to W$ with maximal ideal generated by $\sigma(\varpi)$, power series $f, u, v \in W[[X_0,X_1]]$ with $u, v$ units and $f - (X_0X_1^q - X_0^qX_1) \in (X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$, and a ring isomorphism
--   $$e : \widehat{\mathcal{O}}_{X,z} \;\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\; S := W[[X_0,X_1]]/\big(C(\sigma(\varpi_t^{\,q+1}))\,v - f\,u\big)$$
--   from the adic completion of the stalk at $z$, subject to seven clauses, stated there with $\mathrm{toC}$ the map from the stalk to its completion, $\mathrm{mkS}$ the quotient map onto $S$, and $\mathrm{germY}$ the induced homomorphism $C \to \mathcal{O}_{X,z}$ through `ιFin`: (i) $e$ carries the germ of $a \in A$ to the class of $C(\sigma a)$; (ii) for every $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and every $L$-algebra automorphism $\tau$ of $K$ satisfying [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.IsLevelAutAt L q ζ q (q ^ 2 * M') H₁ γ⁻¹ K τ`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelLevelAutAt.html#L29) — that is, for all weights $k$, all modular forms $f_0, g_0$ of level $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ with integral $q$-expansions and $g_0$ with nonzero series, all $x \in K$ whose Laurent expansion is the ratio of these series, and all $\iota$ with $\iota(\zeta) = \exp(2\pi i/q)$, the series of $\tau x$ multiplied by the $q$-expansion of $g_0\mid_k \mathrm{conjElemN}\,q\,\gamma^{-1}$ equals the $q$-expansion of $f_0\mid_k \mathrm{conjElemN}\,q\,\gamma^{-1}$ — the chart algebra $C$ is stable under $\tau$; (iii) for such $\gamma$ with $\gamma_{11} \equiv 1 \pmod{\ell}$ and such $\tau$ preserving $C$, the restriction of $\tau$ to $C$ is congruent to the identity modulo $y'$; (iv) for such $\gamma$, $\tau$ preserving $C$ and congruent to the identity modulo $y'$, there are a ring automorphism $\theta$ of $S$, an element $c \in W$ and a matrix $M \in M_2(W)$ with: $\theta$ intertwining the action of $\tau$ on $C$ through $e \circ \mathrm{toC} \circ \mathrm{germY}$, $\theta$ fixing the constants $C(w)$, $\theta(X_j) \equiv \sum_i M_{ij}X_i$ modulo the square of the ideal generated by the classes of $X_0, X_1$, $c^{q+1} \equiv 1$, $M_{ij} \equiv c\,\gamma_{ij}$ modulo the maximal ideal of $W$, $c \equiv 1$ whenever $\gamma_{11} \equiv 1 \pmod{\ell}$, and $c \not\equiv 1$ whenever $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau$ is not the identity; (v) separation of branches: for integers $a_1,b_1,a_2,b_2$ and prime ideals $P_1, P_2$ of $S$, each not containing both classes of $X_0$ and $X_1$, each containing the class of $C(\sigma\varpi)$, each containing an element $a_iX_0 + b_iX_1 + h$ with $h \in (X_0,X_1)^2$, and with $q \nmid a_1b_2 - a_2b_1$, the contractions of $P_1$ and $P_2$ along $e$ composed with $\mathrm{toC}$ are distinct; (vi) for a prime $P$ of $S$ with the same nondegeneracy, containing the class of $C(\sigma\varpi)$ and an element $X_0 + h$ with $h \in (X_0,X_1)^2$: for $a \in C$, the image $\mathrm{toC}(\mathrm{germY}(a))$ lies in the contraction of $P$ along $e$ if and only if every Laurent coefficient of $a$ lies in the image of the maximal ideal of $A$; (vii) a Hasse-type clause: [`ModularCurve.jqNModC L q`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18) lies in $K$ and its class lies in $C$, there is $a_0 \in A$ with this class minus $a_0$ in $y'$, and there are $e_0 \ge 1$ and $h \in (X_0,X_1)^{e_0}$ such that for all $a, b \in W$, at least one a unit, with $a^qb - ab^q$ in the maximal ideal, the value $\sum_{i=0}^{e_0} (\text{coefficient of } X_0^iX_1^{e_0-i} \text{ in } h)\,a^ib^{e_0-i}$ is a unit, and $e$ carries $\mathrm{toC}(\mathrm{germY}(\,\cdot\,))$ of that difference to the class of $h$.
--
--   One such point and one such presentation are then fixed: a point $z$ of $X$ with $\varpi_z$ as above (`hϖz`) in the maximal ideal of the stalk (`hz`), a point $y'$ of `XFin` over $z$ (`hy'`) which is supersingular (`hss'`) and whose ideal equals $y$ (`hy'y`); and data $W_1, \sigma_1$ with maximal ideal generated by $\sigma_1(\varpi)$ (`hσ₁`), power series $f_1, u_1, v_1$ with $u_1, v_1$ units (`hu₁`, `hv₁`) and $f_1$ congruent to $X_0X_1^q - X_0^qX_1$ modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$ (`hf₁`), and an isomorphism $e_1$ from the completed stalk at $z$ onto $S := W_1[[X_0,X_1]]/(C(\sigma_1(\varpi_t^{q+1}))v_1 - f_1u_1)$. The hypothesis `hW₁` asserts for these data, at $z$ and $y'$, exactly the seven clauses (i)–(vii) just listed.
--
--   The remaining data are: an ideal $J \subseteq C$ which by `hJ` is the infimum of the set of ideals of the form $\mathrm{comap}_{\tau|_C}\big(\mathrm{comap}_{e_1 \circ \mathrm{toC} \circ \mathrm{germY}}(\text{ideal generated by the classes of } C(\sigma_1\varpi_t),\, X_0,\, X_1)\big)$, taken over all $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and all $\tau$ satisfying `IsLevelAutAt` for $\gamma^{-1}$ and preserving $C$; an $A$-subalgebra $B \subseteq K$ which by `hB` is the algebra generated over $C$ by $\{x \in K : \exists\, i \in J,\ x\varpi_t = i\}$; and a valuation subring $W \subseteq K$ containing $B$ (`hBW`). The hypothesis `hR1` states that $C \le B$ and that every element of $K$ is a ratio of two elements of $B$ with nonzero denominator. The hypothesis `hR2` states that $B$ is formally smooth over $A$, of finite presentation over $A$, and that $B/\varpi B$ has Krull dimension at most $1$. The hypothesis `hR3` has five clauses: an element of $L$ lies in $W$ exactly when it comes from $A$; the maximal ideal of $W$ is generated by $\varpi$; $W$ is a discrete valuation ring; an element of $C$ lies in $y$ exactly when it lies in the maximal ideal of $W$; and $f \in K$ lies in $W$ exactly when $f = g/h$ with $g, h \in B$ and $h$ outside the maximal ideal of $W$.
--
--   The hypothesis `hEQ` has five clauses. First, for every $\mathrm{GF}(q^2)$-algebra structure on the residue field $k_A$ of $A$ there is a surjective ring homomorphism $\rho : B \to$ [`DrinfeldCurve.CoordRing q k_A`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L21) $= k_A[X_0,X_1]/(\mathrm{drinfeldPoly} - 1)$ whose vanishing locus is the maximal ideal of $W$, which is compatible with $A \to k_A$, and which is equivariant: for every $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and every $\tau$ satisfying `IsLevelAutAt` for $\gamma^{-1}$ and preserving $W$, there is $c \in \mathrm{GF}(q^2)^\times$ with $(\mathrm{redQ}\,q\,\gamma, c)$ in [`DrinfeldCurve.hSubgroup q`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L276), the kernel of $(g,c) \mapsto \det(g)\,c^{q+1}$, such that $\rho \circ \tau =$ `hAction` of that element applied to $\rho$, and with $c \ne 1$ whenever $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau$ is not the identity. Second, $B$ is stable under every such $\tau$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$. Third, every prime ideal $Q$ of $B$ containing $\varpi$ is reached by a translate: there are $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and $\tau$ satisfying `IsLevelAutAt` for $\gamma^{-1}$ such that every $b \in B$ lying in the maximal ideal of $W$ has $\tau b \in B$ and $\tau b \in Q$. Fourth, if $b \in B$ is carried into the maximal ideal of $W$ by all such $\tau$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$, then $\varpi \mid b$ in $B$. Fifth, for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and $\tau$ as above, if $\tau$ preserves $y$ on $C$ then $\tau$ preserves $W$.
--
--   Finally, a finite set `ends` of subrings of $K$ is characterised by `hends`: a subring $O$ belongs to `ends` if and only if there is a nonzero $a \in J$ such that, with $B_a$ the $A$-algebra generated over $C$ by $\{x \in K : \exists\, i \in J,\ xa = i\}$, there is a maximal ideal $P$ of $B_a$ with $O$ the localisation of $B_a$ at $P$ (that is, $f \in O$ exactly when $f = g/h$ with $g, h \in B_a$, $h \notin P$), with every element of $y$ a non-unit of $O$, and with $B \not\subseteq O$. The hypothesis `hperm` states that every $\tau$ satisfying `IsLevelAutAt` for $\gamma^{-1}$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and preserving $W$ permutes `ends`: for $O \in$ `ends` there is $O' \in$ `ends` with $f \in O \iff \tau f \in O'$.
--
--   The conclusion is the conjunction of three assertions.
--
--   (1) There is an $A$-subalgebra $B_c \subseteq K$ of finite type over $A$, stable under every $\tau$ satisfying `IsLevelAutAt` for $\gamma^{-1}$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$, such that for every $O \in$ `ends`: $B_c \subseteq O$, and $f \in O$ if and only if there are $g, h \in B_c$ with $h$ a unit of $O$ and $fh = g$. Thus $B_c$ is a single affine chart contained in all the ends, each end being a localisation of it.
--
--   (2) For every $O \in$ `ends`, every $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and every $\tau$ satisfying `IsLevelAutAt` for $\gamma^{-1}$: if $\tau$ maps $O$ into $W$, then $W$ is $\tau$-stable, i.e. $f \in W \iff \tau f \in W$.
--
--   (3) For every valuation subring $V$ of $K$ such that an element of $L$ lies in $V$ exactly when it comes from $A$, such that $C \subseteq V$, and such that every element of $y$ lies in the maximal ideal of $V$: either $B \subseteq V$, or there is $O \in$ `ends` with $O \subseteq V$ and every non-unit of $O$ lying in the maximal ideal of $V$, that is, $V$ dominates $O$.
--
--   This is the local step, at a supersingular closed point of the two-chart integral model, which organises the ends of the blow-up of the supersingular chart: it produces one affine chart common to all ends, shows that a translate carrying an end into the distinguished valuation ring already stabilises that ring, and shows that the blow-up chart together with the ends covers all admissible valuations of $K$. It is stated in the auxiliary frame with full level replaced by the $\Gamma_1(\ell)$-diamond condition for a prime $\ell \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ dividing $M'$, so that it applies for small $q$ as well, and it is used by [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.AuxLevelOne.exists_cyclicDecomposition_ends_moduliHasse_igusaSepTranslate_commonChart_cover_blowupChart_linked_of_eq_adjoin_of_drinfeldChartWitness_of_dvd`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.AuxLevelOne.exists_cyclicDecomposition_ends_moduliHasse_igusaSepTranslate_commonChart_cover_blowupChart_linked_of_eq_adjoin_of_drinfeldChartWitness_of_dvd).
